#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.13"
# dependencies = ["httpx2"]
# ///
"""Watch the GitHub Actions runs on the registry's private repos, redrawn in place.

    watch-ci [-n SECONDS] [-k RUNS] [--repo NAME]... [--once]

Private repos are the ones whose CI runs on the self-hosted runner, so those are
the registry rows it polls.

Each run says what it ran for: the trigger, the pull request it tested or merged,
the tag on its commit, and who started it when that was not the owner. The pull
request comes from the repo's pull request list rather than the run's own
`pull_requests`, because GitHub empties that once the pull request closes. A push
run is matched to the pull request whose merge commit it ran on. Under the title
is the run's URL. It is the one handle a browser and a copy both take whole,
because `gh run view` takes an id and a repo rather than a URL.

Under each running or queued run is one line per job: its runner, its current
step and how long that step has run, or the labels a waiting job needs. A running
run shows the median of its workflow's last successful runs beside its age.

FAILED lists every run with a failed job in any attempt, and under it each failed
job with its attempt. A run's conclusion is its latest attempt's, so a run whose
failed job was rerun, or that was cancelled after a job failed, would otherwise
hide that failure. A failure
whose rerun passed, whose workflow has passed on that branch since, or whose pull
request has closed says so and drops its log excerpt. A standing one shows the
lines of its failed step's log that name the failure, read once per job.

Every GET sends If-None-Match with the last ETag, and GitHub does not count a 304
against the rate limit, so idle repos are free. A 304 touches the cached body, so
its mtime says when GitHub last confirmed it. A running run's jobs change on every
step, so most of those polls are a 200. They refresh every JOBS_EVERY seconds
rather than every interval to stay well inside the hourly limit. Any other run
that is not a first-attempt success is read once after its last update, since its
jobs are then fixed. A repo's pull requests and tags are read when its runs
change, and every REFERENCES_EVERY seconds otherwise.

The screen fits the terminal's height. Running and queued runs are placed first,
then standing failures, then the other finished runs newest first, so a long
FAILED list never pushes a live run off the top.
"""

